patch-manager for Debian
========================

Building the source package
---------------------------
Build the source package from a clean checkout, not a working development
tree: a virtualenv (.venv/), an editor socket or a patman database left in
the tree makes dpkg-source fail.  From a fresh clone of the upstream git
repository:

    tops=$(git ls-tree --name-only HEAD | grep -v '^debian$')
    git archive --prefix=patch-manager-<version>/ \
        -o ../patch-manager_<version>.orig.tar.gz HEAD $tops
    debuild -S

The orig tarball is the upstream tree without the debian/ directory.

Naming
------
Upstream ships on PyPI as "patch-manager"; this source package keeps that
name.  The binary package is called "patman", after the command it provides.

debian/patches
--------------
0001-drop-pep639-license-metadata.patch removes the [project] license keys
from pyproject.toml for the build.  Debian and Ubuntu stable ship a
setuptools that predates PEP 639 and rejects the SPDX license string, while
very new setuptools is phasing out the classic table form; dropping the keys
builds across both.  debian/copyright is the authoritative licensing.

The AI review feature
---------------------
patman's optional "review" command needs the claude-agent-sdk, which is not
packaged in Debian; it is therefore not a dependency and that feature is
unavailable in the packaged tool.  Everything else -- creating, checking and
sending patches with git send-email or a web relay, and tracking a series on
Patchwork -- works normally.
